ow substrings simultaneously: the canonical step name `- name: External live-Dokploy smoke tests`, the canonical run command `go test -run TestLiveDokploySmoke ./...`, the `workflow_dispatch:` trigger, the `schedule:` trigger, and the `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY: "1"` env injection. Pinning the env-injection literal is essential: without it, the smoke would t.Skip silently and the workflow would always report green without ever reaching Dokploy. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: pin BOTH triggers AND the env-injection literal in the same matcher; the trigger + opt-in pair is what makes the workflow's nightly run actually exercise the gate. (3) `VERIFY.SH OPTIONAL STEP GATED ON THE SAME ENV VAR THE TEST READS AT RUNTIME` — the verify.sh step's `if [[ "${YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY:-}" = "1" ]]; then` guard ensures the optional step is skipped on developer laptops without the opt-in, but exercises the identical command CI runs when the operator does opt in. The static test pins BOTH the canonical step header `# 29. Optional: external live-Dokploy smoke tests` AND the literal command `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1 go test -run TestLiveDokploySmoke ./...` — the leading env prefix is part of the contract because verify.sh's step header is a comment, not an env-injection line, so the env prefix MUST appear in the command itself. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: gate the verify.sh optional step on the SAME env var the test reads at runtime and pin the env-prefixed command literal as part of the contract. (4) `CONTRIBUTING.md ENTRY LIVES IN A NEW "Opt-in external suites" SUBSECTION UNDER THE EXISTING OPTIONAL TOOLS BLOCK, NOT THE NUMBERED REQUIRED LIST` — the numbered required list (1-24 after BE-0399) is reserved for the on-every-PR gates; opt-in suites get a separate subsection so contributors see the cadence boundary at a glance. The static test pins only the canonical entry literal (the command with surrounding backticks) so the analyser is robust to Markdown line wrap and prose changes around it. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: add the entry to the Optional Tools subsection (creating it if needed), and pin only the canonical command literal in the static test, not the surrounding prose. (5) `SECURITY.md ROW's When COLUMN SAYS "Opt-in (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1`), nightly + manual", NOT THE UNIFORM "Every push and PR" USED BY EVERY OTHER GATE` — the When column is the operator-facing signal that distinguishes the opt-in cadence from the on-every-PR cadence. The static test pins the entire row literal so a regression that flipped the cadence to "Every push and PR" would fail the matcher; a regression that dropped the row entirely would also fail. The dedicated `## External Live-Dokploy Smoke Tests` section's 16 substring set includes a NEW invariant set distinct from BE-0399: the documented env vars (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY, YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY_BASE_URL, YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY_TOKEN), the cadence framing (opt-in, nightly), the canonical workflow path (.github/workflows/external-smoke.yml), the typed error code surface (yalla.error.v1), the redaction contract (redacted), the canonical function pair, and the self-locating static-test filename. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: pin the When-column literal AND a dedicated section with env-var + cadence + workflow-path substrings. Load-bearing invariant unchanged from BE-0379..BE-0399: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. ALSO: the smoke test's canonical pair uses a NON-CONTENTION shape (closed-set opt-in flag values + live-path-with-deterministic-redaction-branch) instead of the closed-set-coverage + per-decision-stability-burst shape of BE-0379..BE-0399 — appropriate for a SMOKE whose "live" branch is by definition external, where firing thousands of goroutines at a real Dokploy would be hostile to the upstream and meaningless under the race detector. Forward-applicable to every smoke / opt-in story whose live path is external infrastructure: drop the contention burst, replace it with a deterministic redaction-contract branch (httptest.NewServer that echoes a sentinel token; assert the typed wire error AND the absence of the token in the error chain) so the redaction property is enforced on every push and PR even when the live path is skipped. ALSO: the Dokploy client maps a 401/403 from the upstream to `apierr.Internal()` → `yerr.CodeInternal` (Yalla's OWN credentials rejected = internal Yalla misconfiguration, NOT customer-actionable auth failure) — pinning this in the synthetic-server branch is itself a load-bearing contract worth pinning across any future Dokploy-touching smoke.
